{# Four things about the airport itself, then where it is.

   Not the city's facts over again -- the country and the city are both in the
   trail above, one click each. Altitude is here instead because it is the one
   fact in this data that belongs to the airport and to nothing else: El Alto is
   at 13,313 feet and Schiphol is 11 below the sea.

   Feet, which is what the column holds. Checked against airports whose
   elevation is known rather than assumed: Denver reads 5,389, Bogota 8,361,
   Amsterdam -11. The card on /airports has been calling these metres.

   The time zone is printed from the stored offset through gmt_offset(), because
   the column is a decimal and eight of these airports are on a half hour --
   raw it says "GMT+5.5" and rounded it lies to everyone flying to India. #}
{% include 'partials/facts-grid.html.twig' with {
    'facts': [
        { 'icon': 'fa-location-dot', 'value': place.city, 'label': 'City' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-plane', 'value': place.code, 'label': 'IATA code' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-clock', 'value': gmt_offset(place.timezone), 'label': 'Time zone' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-mountain', 'value': place.altitude|number_format ~ ' ft', 'label': 'Altitude' }
    ]
} only %}

{# No zoom named here. One point cannot be framed, so how far out a single-pin
   map opens is one decision for the whole site and it lives in
   config/common/maps.php -- see MapView::zoomFor(). What this map is for is
   showing which part of the city the airport sits in, and what is around it. #}
{% include 'partials/map.html.twig' with {
    'markers': [{ 'lat': place.latitude, 'lon': place.longitude }],
    'label': 'Map showing where ' ~ place.title ~ ' sits in ' ~ place.city
} only %}
